All three deliverables are in place and verified.

**What was produced**

- `/app/active_users_report.json` — 346 active users (of 500; the rest are inactive/pending), 2-space indented array.
- `/app/cmd.sh` — the exact jq command with `--indent 2`, the filter, `/app/users.json` as input and `> /app/active_users_report.json` redirection; re-running it reproduces the output byte-identically.
- `/app/jq_args.yaml` — exactly two keys, `filter` (single string) and `args` (`["--indent 2"]`). Running the specified construction `jq $(yq -r '.args[]' ...) "$(yq -r '.filter' ...)" /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json` yields a file identical to the reference, so indentation is guaranteed through the args path (`--indent 2` rather than a multi-line filter inside the shell-quoted string).

**Checks that passed against the source data**

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| Output ids == set of `status=="active"` ids | exact match |
| `last_login` = date part of source timestamp, all `YYYY-MM-DD` | 0 mismatches, regex all true |
| `role_count` and `primary_role` (null when empty) vs source roles | 0 mismatches; 50 nulls = 50 active users with empty roles |
| `user_id`/`username`/`email` fidelity | 0 mismatches |
| Sorted ascending by username | true |
| Valid JSON, keys exactly the six required | true |

